        content: "There is no single required DSCR. The minimum depends on the property type, the lender, the market, and the strength of the borrower. But the ranges below are the ones lenders commonly work with for stabilized properties, and they are a good starting point for any first deal.\n\n| Property type | Typical minimum DSCR |\n|---|---|\n| Multifamily (5+ units) | 1.20x\u20131.25x |\n| Industrial / warehouse | 1.20x\u20131.30x |\n| Retail | 1.25x\u20131.40x |\n| Office | 1.30x\u20131.45x |\n| Hospitality (hotels) | 1.40x\u20131.55x |\n\nMultifamily has the lowest floor because its income is spread across many units and demand is relatively steady. Office and hotels sit at the top because their income is more volatile \u2014 office leases roll over in large blocks, and hotel income can swing with the season and the economy.\n\nA few program benchmarks worth knowing:\n\n- **Agency multifamily (Fannie Mae / Freddie Mac):** Fannie Mae's conventional multifamily term sheets list a minimum DSCR of 1.25x. That is why 1.25x is the number you will hear most often in apartment deals.\n- **Agency affordable-housing and green programs:** published program summaries put these lower, around 1.15x\u20131.20x, in exchange for affordability or efficiency commitments.\n- **SBA 504 and 7(a) loans:** for owner-occupied commercial property, lender guidance typically points to a minimum DSCR of around 1.15x\u20131.25x, depending on the lender and the borrower's overall financial picture.\n\nTreat every number above as a typical range, not a guarantee. A strong sponsor with a long track record can sometimes borrow at a lower DSCR; a weak market, a single-tenant property, or a loan with near-term lease expirations can push the requirement higher. The only number that matters for your deal is the one your lender quotes \u2014 confirm it before you go under contract.",
