        title: "How to Improve Your DSCR",
        content: "DSCR has only three levers: raise income, cut expenses, or reduce the debt service. Using the apartment building from the worked example \u2014 NOI $250,000, debt service $180,000, DSCR 1.39x \u2014 here is what each lever does.\n\n**Raise the income.** A $150-per-month rent increase across all 20 units adds $36,000 a year to gross income. After the 5% vacancy allowance, NOI rises by about $34,200, to roughly $284,000. DSCR becomes $284,200 \u00f7 $180,000 = **1.58x**. Rent increases are the most powerful lever, but only if the market supports them \u2014 check comparable rents before assuming the increase sticks, and remember a lender will want to see the higher rents actually collected before crediting them.\n\n**Cut operating expenses.** Reducing expenses by $12,000 a year \u2014 renegotiating insurance, cutting a wasteful service contract, fixing a leak that was driving up water bills \u2014 raises NOI to $262,000. DSCR becomes $262,000 \u00f7 $180,000 = **1.46x**. Expense cuts work immediately, but do not cut maintenance that protects the building's value; deferred maintenance shows up later as capital spending or lower rents.\n\n**Reduce the debt service.** A larger down payment means a smaller loan and a smaller payment. A longer amortization schedule spreads the same loan over more years and lowers the annual payment. Refinancing to a lower rate does the same. For example, if the monthly payment fell from $15,000 to $13,500, annual debt service would drop to $162,000, and DSCR would rise to $250,000 \u00f7 $162,000 = **1.54x**. The trade-off is real: longer amortization means slower equity build-up and more total interest paid, so weigh the higher DSCR against the long-term cost.\n\nThe honest version of \"improving DSCR\" is improving the property or the loan \u2014 not reworking the spreadsheet. Lenders underwrite to their own income assumptions, so a DSCR that only exists in your model will not survive their review.",
