        title: "How balloon loans work",
        content: "Balloon structures are written as term over amortization: a \"5/25\" is a 5-year term on a 25-year schedule; \"7/25\" and \"10/25\" work the same way. During the term you make ordinary monthly payments, sized as though the loan ran the full amortization period. At maturity the remaining balance comes due in full \u2014 there is no final small payment that clears it.\n\nTwo details catch first-time borrowers out. First, the rate is usually fixed only for the term, so a refinance at maturity reprices at whatever rates are then \u2014 the payment above is the payment for this loan, not a promise about the next one. Second, some balloon loans are interest-only, so the balance never falls and the balloon equals the full original loan amount. Check whether your loan amortizes; the note will say so.",
