        content: "Run the identical math to year ten.\n\n**Buy side.** Mortgage payments over 120 months total $798,110. The loan balance falls to $717,458, so you have repaid $182,542 of principal and paid $615,568 of interest. Total cash out is the $300,000 down payment, plus $36,000 of purchase costs, plus $798,110 of mortgage payments, plus $180,000 of operating costs \u2014 **$1,314,110**. Selling at the same flat $1,200,000, less 6% selling costs ($72,000) and the $717,458 payoff, returns **$410,542**. Net cost of buying for ten years:\n\n$1,314,110 \u2212 $410,542 = **$903,568**\n\n**Lease side.** Rent in years 6\u201310 adds $553,927, bringing the ten-year total to **$1,031,749**.\n\n**Ten-year verdict: buying wins by $128,181** ($1,031,749 \u2212 $903,568).\n\nThe flip happens during year 7. At the end of year 6, leasing is still ahead by $19,783. By the end of year 7, buying is ahead by $9,828, and the gap keeps widening. Two forces drive it: principal paydown ($182,542 by year 10) finally exceeds the $108,000 of transaction costs, and rent keeps compounding upward while the mortgage payment stays fixed at $6,651.\n\n| Hold period | Cost of buying | Cost of leasing | Winner |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 5 years | $522,648 | $477,822 | Lease by $44,826 |\n| 10 years | $903,568 | $1,031,749 | Buy by $128,181 |",
